These capabilities provide a foundation for organising field information. The workflow below shows how a rail team can use that foundation; the exact configuration and available AI functions should be confirmed for the deployment.

1. Define the inspection around the work
Start with the activity, location and controls that need checking. A workshop inspection should reflect its current tasks. A station inspection should account for temporary arrangements as well as routine conditions.

Give inspectors a way to record unexpected concerns. Review templates when the work changes rather than allowing yesterday’s questions to become a permanent substitute for observation.

2. Capture evidence that another person can understand
A helpful finding tells the reviewer what was observed, where it occurred, who may be exposed and which control needs attention. It also records the activity underway and any immediate response.

“Area unsafe” is difficult to act on. “Delivery materials narrowing the marked pedestrian route beside Workshop B during unloading” gives the next person something specific to investigate. A suitable photograph and location reference add context.

Offline capture can help teams record findings in low-connectivity areas. Urgent concerns still need the operator’s immediate communication and escalation route. An unsynchronised record is not evidence that the responsible person has been notified.

3. Review patterns without losing the detail
NeoEHS describes AI-supported analysis of inspection records, observations and action history. The practical opportunity is to bring recurring themes to a reviewer’s attention.

The reviewer should inspect the source records, check whether the circumstances are comparable and decide what further investigation is needed. An AI suggestion should remain distinguishable from a confirmed finding.

A location with many reports may have more activity or a stronger reporting culture. Compare the context before labelling it the least safe site.

4. Give every action a clear owner
Once a finding is reviewed, specify the required response, responsible person and due time appropriate to the risk. Keep temporary controls and unresolved concerns visible during handover.

Where an inspection reveals an incident or near miss, use the organisation’s investigation process. NeoEHS Incident Management provides a related workflow for reporting and investigation; inspection closure should not replace that process.

5. Verify what changed
Action completion and action effectiveness answer different questions. Completion asks whether the agreed task was done. Effectiveness asks whether it addressed the problem.

For a recurring condition, check again during the relevant activity. If materials obstructed a route during deliveries, a quiet-period photograph may offer little evidence that the revised arrangement works.

The UK Health and Safety Executive’s guidance on managing risk emphasises reviewing controls, including when work changes or problems are reported. This is a useful management reference; local rail rules and jurisdiction-specific duties still govern the operation.

