What computer vision can add
Scheduled inspections cannot observe every activity continuously. NeoEHS AI Computer Vision describes monitoring for configured visual conditions, including missing PPE and entry into restricted zones, with alerts for review.

Before deployment, define the particular condition, camera coverage and response owner. Test the setup under the lighting, weather, obstructions and working practices it will encounter. Record missed detections as well as false alerts, and agree how the team will recognise a loss of coverage.

Visual monitoring cannot confirm an electrical isolation, assess every hidden defect or authorise train movement. It should complement established controls within a clearly defined scope.

An illustrative example: a finding that returns every week
A depot team repeatedly finds materials beside a marked access route. Each report is closed after the materials are moved. The next delivery creates the same condition.

A combined review of the records prompts the team to observe the receiving process. They identify an unsuitable temporary storage arrangement and consider a revised unloading plan with the people doing the work.

The team assigns the agreed changes, records implementation and checks subsequent deliveries. If the obstruction returns, the action is reopened and the arrangement is reviewed again.

This is an illustrative scenario, not a NeoEHS customer case study. It demonstrates a review approach without claiming a measured outcome.

How to evaluate an AI inspection pilot
Choose a manageable area and one problem that matters to the operational team. Avoid beginning with a network-wide rollout or an assumption that every finding needs AI analysis.

Agree the question. For example: can reviewers identify repeated access-route findings more consistently?
Define the evidence. Specify mandatory locations, activity details and finding categories.
Set responsibilities. Name who reviews suggestions, responds to urgent issues and verifies closure.
Establish a baseline. Record the existing review process, workload and known gaps.
Compare with competent review. Check a representative sample, including records the AI did not flag.
Review usefulness and limitations. Examine incorrect groupings, missed patterns and the effort needed to resolve them.
Decide the next step. Refine the setup, expand a validated use case or stop an unhelpful application.
The NIST AI Risk Management Framework provides a voluntary reference for evaluating AI trustworthiness and managing risk. It is not a certification of NeoEHS or a replacement for a rail operator’s own assurance process.

Measure inspection effectiveness, not just volume
Useful measures include high-priority findings awaiting action, reporting-to-acknowledgement time, repeat findings by activity, reopened actions and closure records with the required evidence.

Define the period and denominator for each measure. For example, a reopening rate needs an agreed cohort of closed actions and a consistent follow-up window. Without those definitions, comparisons can be misleading.

Use inspection counts as an activity measure. Assess control effectiveness through evidence and competent review. A lower number of reports alone does not demonstrate safer work.

