Frequently asked questions
Can AI replace a qualified railway safety inspector?
No. AI can support record analysis and selected visual monitoring. Competent people still inspect conditions, assess the evidence and make authorised safety decisions.

Can NeoEHS support inspections without an internet connection?
NeoEHS describes offline inspection capture with synchronisation when connectivity returns. Confirm the supported devices and workflow during implementation. Urgent hazards require a separate immediate communication route.

Does every AI-powered inspection require CCTV?
No. Analysis of inspection records and corrective-action history is distinct from camera-based monitoring. Computer vision is a separate use case that requires suitable coverage, configuration and validation.

Can this software certify track, signalling or rolling-stock safety?
This article concerns EHS inspection management. Do not treat its workflows or AI outputs as substitutes for prescribed technical testing, certification or authorised rail engineering decisions.

What makes inspection data useful for AI analysis?
Consistent location and activity information, clear finding descriptions, suitable evidence and reliable action history make records easier to compare. Missing context and inconsistent labels can weaken the analysis.

Where should a metro or railway operator start?
Select one operational area and a recurring inspection problem. Agree the evidence, response owners and evaluation criteria, then test usefulness before expanding the scope.

Make inspection findings easier to act on
The purpose of an inspection is to help people understand conditions and improve controls. A useful system makes the evidence accessible, the responsibility clear and the response reviewable.

